{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794444201,"nonce":"hLS4Sq9aU4p5JG+BdB3HZP9AzdY=","message":"Committed as a63c5aa, not pushed.\n\nBytes and objects to a lent function: `impl FnMut(\u0026[u8], Counter) -\u003e Result\u003c(), E\u003e`.\n\nBytes go as text does: a `Slice` borrowed for the call. The hxcpp trampoline copies them into a `haxe.io.BytesData` (`hxn::bytes`); HashLink's copies them into the VM's bytes and boxes those and their length, and the wrapper makes the `haxe.io.Bytes`. Bytes are not given back by a lent function yet.\n\nAn object is given, not lent. `impl Fn(\u0026Counter)` is refused: the Haxe object made for it could be kept past the call, with nothing behind it. So the function takes `T`, or `Arc\u003cT\u003e` of a shared interface, and the host owns the value from then on, as it owns one a function returns. The adapter's closure turns the value into the handle the host gets (`Handle::into_raw`, `manage`), and the Haxe wrapper makes the object and takes ownership before it calls the user's function, so a function that throws leaves an object that is collected like any other.\n\nHow the pointer reaches the wrapper:\n- hxcpp: the trampoline passes `cpp::Pointer\u003cvoid\u003e`, and the wrapper takes `cpp.Pointer\u003ccpp.Void\u003e` and sets the new object's `raw` from it.\n- HashLink: a closure is called with boxed arguments the VM converts to the closure's parameter types, and native code has no type to box a `counter_counter` abstract as. The pointer is boxed as bytes. The first version had the wrapper `cast` those bytes to the abstract, which Ash's hybrid and interpreter modes let through and stock HashLink 1.15 and Ash's JIT refuse (\"Can't cast bytes to counter_counter\"). So each interface that is given to a lent function has one more primitive, `\u003clibrary\u003e_\u003cinterface\u003e_adopt_lent`, declared as taking bytes and returning the abstract, which returns its argument; the class's `adoptLent` calls it.\n\nTwo shapes that differ only in which interface an object is of are one shape at the boundary, so `lent::used` is now distinct by a shape's letters and not by its types.\n\nFixture: `Counter::chunks`, `Counter::spawn_each`, `Pot::offer`.\n\nNot built: bytes or an object given back by a lent function; an `Option` of anything; `i64`.\n\nRun: tests/counter/run.sh gen-hl on Ash (hybrid, jit, interp, three times each) and stock HashLink on the NUC, gen-cpp, gen-cppia, gen-wasm, gen-emcc, the hand-written hl, cpp and emcc modes, and tests/examples/run.sh on Ash and hxcpp. Not run: Windows and hxcpp on Linux.","files":null}]}